Actually, my patch, as I have it now, does hold the player responsible
for their pet killing a formerly peaceful creature.  Thus, if a player
attacks a peaceful creature (or otherwise makes it hostile), and the
pet kills it, the player is penalized.  The act which changes the
creature to hostile is player controlled, and thus they are held
responsible.  I'm not 100% sure that I want the penalties to be
equivalent, especially since the player doesn't get the experience for
the kill to offset them, so my current version has slightly lower
penalties.

Of course, what a monk's pet eats does not change whether or not he
himself is a vegetarian--I've yet to meet a vegetarian (or vegan) who
would starve their dog because of the lack of vegetarian/vegan dogfood
on the market; that argument is rather pointless.  Your knight example
is better; perhaps I will put in checks for those sorts of situations
as well.  Thanks for reminding me of them.

